<section id="introduction" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="introduction">Introduction</h2>
<p>In this blog, I am looking at the <a href="https://github.com/rfordatascience/tidytuesday/blob/ecf1b9a106523e4b786e53d2b2b0ff370b809408/data/2023/2023-02-07/readme.md">Big Tech Stock Prices</a> data set (originally sourced from <a href="https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/evangower/big-tech-stock-prices">Kaggle</a>), which is a comprehensive collection of the daily stock prices and volume of 14 different tech companies, including Apple, Amazon, and Meta. The data details how these tech giants fared from 2010 to 2022.</p>
<p>My exploration is based around on the idea of risk vs.&nbsp;reward, and centers on the question: <strong>Which tech stocks are the most volatile, and how do their daily price distributions differ?</strong> To answer this, I am focusing on three primary variables:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>adj_close</code> is the closing price after adjustments for all applicable splits and dividend distributions, which I use to track the baseline value of the stocks.</p></li>
<li><p><code>open</code> and <code>close</code> are the the price at market open and the price at market close (adjusted for splits), respectively, and I use them to calculate the daily percentage fluctuations that define volatility.</p></li>
<li><p><code>stock_symbol</code> represents the full name of the associated company, which I need to group and compare trends and happenings across the companies in the data set.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>This is a fairly large data set, containing 45,088 observations. An important contextual note for the reader is that all companies have 3,271 prices, excepting Meta which has 2,688, and Tesla which has 3,148 because they were not publicly traded for part of the period examined in the data.</p>
</section>
