<p>To look at daily risk, I use faceted boxplots to visualize the distribution of daily returns (the percentage change from open to close). This visualization reveals the internal stability of these stocks. Each boxplot shows the range of a company’s typical daily gain or loss; the central boxes show where the price usually settles and the outliers represent extreme trading days. These are moments where the stock price moved significantly more than usual, indicating which companies are more prone to sudden price shocks.</p>
<p>The key takeaway here is the consistency of the stock: a more compact box indicates a stable, predictable asset, while a wider box with a lot of outliers identifies a stock prone to sudden fluctuations in price. Readers should note that the scales for each panel are not the same across the grid – they are relative to each individual boxplot.</p>
