</div>
<p>This final plot defines the risk ranking by sorting these companies based on their median absolute daily returns. The dashed line represents the sector median, marking the middle point of volatility for this group of companies to provide a clear point of reference for comparison.</p>
<p>This visualization should enable readers to identify the sector’s stable points versus its more unpredictable points. Stocks further to the right of the dashed line (sector median) are objectively more volatile than others more to the left, so they require a higher risk tolerance. This ranking helps investors to see exactly where a company like Tesla (TSLA) or NVIDIA is on a scale of risk compared to established companies like Microsoft (MSFT) or Google (AAPL).</p>
</section>
<section id="conclusion" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
<p>A potential flaw in my exploration is that I used <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cfrac%7Bclose%20-%20open%7D%7Bopen%7D"> to calculate the daily return variable, but this only accounts for what happens with prices from open to close . The formula doesn’t consider what happens with prices when the market is closed, which could be significant.</p>
<p>With additional data and more time, I would be interested in including additional data to support and contextualize the the adjusting closing price trends. This could include relevant news, social media mentions, and other supporting data that might help explain what else was going on during extreme market days (outliers in Figures 2 and 3) and <em>why</em> they happened.</p>
</section>
<section id="connection-to-class-ideas" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="connection-to-class-ideas">Connection to Class Ideas</h2>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Data-to-Ink Ratio:</strong> I aimed to minimize data-to-ink ratio in my visualizations by making the plots simple but informative, so they are better remembered and more enjoyable for viewers.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Appropriate Color Scales:</strong> I used a qualitative color scale to distinguish between companies because they are unordered categories.</p></li>
</ul>


</section>

 ]]></description>
